{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Reliance's Working Capital Schedule: Growth Cash Trap",
  "scenario": "You're a junior analyst on the Mumbai desk covering Reliance Industries. Your senior has asked you to build a working capital schedule for Reliance's next three-year forecast, computing Days Inventory Outstanding (DIO), Days Sales Outstanding (DSO), and Days Payable Outstanding (DPO) from historical actuals, then forecasting the rupee balances and Cash Conversion Cycle. The question: is Reliance's projected 12% revenue growth going to devour cash or generate it?",
  "instructions": "Compute historical DIO, DSO, DPO from the Data sheet actuals; forecast these days (flat or with judgment); convert back to rupee balances; calculate net working capital and its year-on-year change; compute the Cash Conversion Cycle. Identify whether the forecast CCC is lengthening (growth is hungry) or shortening (business getting more efficient).",
  "learning_objective": "Build a days-based working capital schedule for Reliance, compute the Cash Conversion Cycle, and interpret whether projected growth will tie up or release cash.",
  "hints": [
    "DIO = Inventory ÷ COGS × 365; DSO = Receivables ÷ Revenue × 365; DPO = Payables ÷ COGS × 365. Compute these for each historical year first.",
    "Once you have the days, forecast them flat (or with your judgment) in the Estimate columns. Then reverse: Inventory = DIO ÷ 365 × COGS. Change in Net WC = this year's Net WC − prior year's Net WC.",
    "CCC = DIO + DSO − DPO. If CCC is rising, growth is tying up more cash per rupee of sales. If negative, suppliers are funding the business (like D-Mart). Check that Change in Net WC flows logically to the cash flow statement."
  ],
